WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.585 align:start size:3% position:97% line:0%
♪

00:00:02.585 --> 00:00:06.840
Birds make non-iridescent
structural colors

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.968
with combinations of
proteins and air

00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:12.887
within particular cells
of their feather barbs.

00:00:12.887 --> 00:00:16.766 align:start size:3% position:97% line:0%
♪

00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:21.813 align:end position:100%
(Vinod)
I was studying how birds like

00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:25.567 align:end position:100%
Blue Jays and Scrub Jays
and Kingfishers,

00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:28.528
they make these kind of
blue colors

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:31.239
that doesn't shimmer,
that is not metallic.

00:00:31.239 --> 00:00:32.949
We call it
non-iridescent.

00:00:32.949 --> 00:00:34.325 align:end position:100%
(Richard)
In previous work,

00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:38.413 align:end position:100%
we discovered that these
materials are self-assembled.

00:00:38.413 --> 00:00:40.999
They come in two "flavors"
or shapes if you will,

00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:43.418
one is spherical like
the bubbles in beer,

00:00:43.418 --> 00:00:45.336
and the other is
kind of twisted

00:00:45.336 --> 00:00:48.798
like the swirls in a bowl
of miso soup.

00:00:48.798 --> 00:00:51.843
When Vinod was a
graduate student in my lab,

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:54.637
we wanted to characterize
all the

00:00:54.637 --> 00:00:56.723
non-iridescent structurally
colored birds of the world

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:59.559
by whether or not they were
spherical or channel-like

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:01.436
in their nanostructure.

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:05.565
(Vinod)
One of the important

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:10.153
aspects of my research is using
collections-based specimens.

00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:11.362
(Richard)
That meant putting

00:01:11.362 --> 00:01:13.948
a lot of feathers in
the x-ray beam,

00:01:13.948 --> 00:01:16.367
looking at how they
scatter the x-rays,

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:18.995
and reconstructing aspects of
their nanostructure

00:01:18.995 --> 00:01:20.497
from those scattered particles.

00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:21.998
(Vinod)
I was going through the drawers

00:01:21.998 --> 00:01:23.708
of the Peabody Museum,

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:25.084
drawer by drawer,

00:01:25.084 --> 00:01:27.253
and I came across these
really interesting birds

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:28.588
called Leafbirds.

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:31.716
They are normally found in South
and Southeast Asia.

00:01:31.716 --> 00:01:33.343
(Richard)
Unlike all of

00:01:33.343 --> 00:01:35.428
the bubbles in beer type,
the spherical,

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:37.764
or the miso soup,
the channel-types,

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:40.308
that we identified before,

00:01:40.308 --> 00:01:43.353
we had something new,
we had a crystalline structure.

00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:45.355
A structure with multiple peaks

00:01:45.355 --> 00:01:47.565
in different and
unexpected places.

00:01:47.565 --> 00:01:51.528
The experiments are
so well controlled,

00:01:51.528 --> 00:01:53.404
the apparatus is so
beautifully structured,

00:01:53.404 --> 00:01:56.366
that when you get these results,
you know it's not an error.

00:01:56.366 --> 00:01:59.577
You know that immediately,
you've got something new.

00:01:59.577 --> 00:02:01.454
What's interesting in
other research

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:04.082
we've looked at
these in butterflies.

00:02:04.082 --> 00:02:07.877
So it turns out these particular
peaks come in certain ratios

00:02:07.877 --> 00:02:11.172
and so we were
well prepared

00:02:11.172 --> 00:02:14.384
to explore: "wait a minute, what
kind of crystals are these?"

00:02:14.384 --> 00:02:21.099
and to identify this material
as a gyroid.

00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:26.187
(Richard)
A gyroid is a shape,

00:02:26.187 --> 00:02:28.439
that has a lot of
special properties.

00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:32.110
At every point on
the surface of a gyroid,

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:33.862
it has the shape of a saddle

00:02:33.862 --> 00:02:38.616
and that's occuring on every
point on the surface of a gyroid

00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:40.118
(Vinod)
Coming to the actual structure

00:02:40.118 --> 00:02:41.494
that we discovered,

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:44.122
it's called a single gyroid.

00:02:44.122 --> 00:02:47.333
Single gyroids normally do
not naturally occur.

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:50.461
The birds, they're making
the single gyroid directly

00:02:50.461 --> 00:02:55.008
and that offers a new way
completely new to physics.

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:56.843
(Richard)
I am really into this

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:59.512
because I care about birds.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.890
But what's fascinating is that
this discovery

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.685
has huge implications
for technology

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:07.729
and for the practical world.

00:03:07.729 --> 00:03:09.105
(Vinod)
The single gyroid

00:03:09.105 --> 00:03:13.651
has superior electronic and
optical properties.

00:03:13.651 --> 00:03:15.403
So that means that this
structure,

00:03:15.403 --> 00:03:17.488
if you can make it large enough,

00:03:17.488 --> 00:03:20.491
they can interact with sunlight
to trap the light.

00:03:20.491 --> 00:03:21.743
(Richard)
Kind of a century after

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:23.286
the electronic revolution,

00:03:23.286 --> 00:03:26.539
we're poised for another
technological revolution

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:28.082
in photonics.

00:03:28.082 --> 00:03:32.003
So what that requires is
controlling the flow of light.

00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:35.673
These optical structures that
birds are using to make colors,

00:03:35.673 --> 00:03:38.343
are also the kinds of structures
you could use

00:03:38.343 --> 00:03:40.345
to manipulate the flow of light

00:03:40.345 --> 00:03:43.598
that allow you the development
of photonic technology.

00:03:43.598 --> 00:03:46.267
The birds provide
an answer to that

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:47.894
because they create
the conditions

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:51.064
under which these materials
make themselves.

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:54.400
So that implies
a very practical line

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.112
for technologists to pursue:

00:03:58.112 --> 00:04:01.699
Self-assembling,
photonic scale,

00:04:01.699 --> 00:04:04.035
single network gyroids
in a lab.

00:04:04.035 --> 00:04:06.454
Because the birds can do it,

00:04:06.454 --> 00:04:09.123
we know,
we must be able to as well.

00:04:09.123 --> 00:04:12.502 align:start size:3% position:97% line:0%
♪

00:04:30.186 --> 00:04:33.147 align:start size:6% position:94% line:0%
♪♪

